    "turn_text": "Leah, thank you so much for sharing your story, your energy, your knowledge, and most importantly, your time, which I know is incredibly precious right now. If you want to keep following her, you can find her on Instagram at greengirllia. If you want to help folks or if you need help, mutuala.org has a really extensive resource library that they're continually updating. We'll link that in the show notes. We'll be right back. You know, here at Undistracted, we are all about staying undistracted. Finding the balance between being informed and empathetic and open and, you know, just basically staying sane. If you love Undistracted, you are going to love the media newsletter. Three times a week, it lands in your inbox with enlightening, surprising, and occasionally hilarious perspective on news, culture, sports, and the fight for gender and racial justice. You'll get exclusive interviews with women and non-binary people making news, plus elders we look up to like Anita Hill and Gloria Steinem. You'll hear writers like Julian Escobedo Shepherd, Rebecca Carroll, and Jessica Valenti dissect the news, plus a little behind the scenes of what's happening here at Undistracted. So if you're not already subscribed, what are you waiting for? Head to wearethemedia.com slash newsletters or head to our show notes and sign up for the newsletter. Trust me, your inbox deserves it. That's wearethemedia.com slash newsletters or the show notes on this episode. You can thank me later. All right, y'all, this week has been, let's say, a source of heartache and mistrust for some of those people, some of y'all put in office. Between the TikTok ban and the incoming presidency, it can feel is that we have no one left in our corner fighting for us, except for maybe Michelle Obama, because she's skipping this inauguration too. But honestly, we still got a lot of people fighting for us. And while some might see these outcomes as a sign that progressive ideas are losing momentum, others see them as a call to find and execute new avenues of justice that will help us survive, not just these next four years, but help us thrive in the days to come. Cory Bush is an activist, nurse, and former U.S. representative. She was elected to Congress for my home state and district, the Missouri 1st, in 2021. And she served for four years before, unfortunately, losing her primary last year. She and I have known each other for years, and as we unpack the current state and future of progressive politics, her knowledge inside and outside of Washington is truly invaluable. My forever Congresswoman, Cory Bush, how are you? It's good to see you. You too. Thank",
